| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer by defining gender budgeting and highlight the increased allocations therein. <br>• In the body, first identify the challenges hindering the effective implementation of gender-sensitive policies and schemes. <br>• Next, suggest some corrective measures. <br>• Conclude by highlighting the importance of implementing these measures. |
| --- |

**Gender budgeting** refers to the systematic **examination of budget, programmes and policies** and their subsequent **impact on women**. It aims at **gender equality** and **women empowerment**. Introduced for the **first time in 2005-06**, it has seen substantial **increase in allocations** over the years (₹4,49,028.68 in FY26, a 37% increase from FY25). However, its **impact on women’s empowerment has been limited** in the country due to the following reasons:
1. a) Greater focus on **consumption-oriented schemes (**e.g. subsidies, maternity benefits) as compared to **structural interventions (**asset ownership, labour force participation);
   b) **blind-spots** in gender budgeting due to neglect of **intersectionality of gender issues** (e.g. issues of tribal women);
   c) failure to capture **invisibilized vulnerabilities (**e.g. transgenders) due to binary notions of gender.

2. a) Grossly **inadequate allocation** (little over 1% of GDP, ~9% of total budget);
   b) **exclusion of vital sectors** of direct importance for women (e.g. unpaid care economy) and **non-comprehensive coverage** (only 49 ministries/departments reported allocations under gender budget);
   c) paucity of quality **gender** **disaggregated data** in crucial sectors hampers effective **baseline assessment** and **impact** **measurement**.

3. a) **Inadequate participation** of women's organizations and **civil society** in budget formulation and review processes;
   b) **limited integration** between different women-centric schemes leading to **fragmented** **service delivery**;
   c) institutional gaps in implementation (e.g. lack of **trained personnel** and **technical** **capacity**).

4. a) Ambiguity resulting from the **A,B,C categorization** (e.g. gender-neutral schemes like PMAY in Part-A);
   b) **larger allocation to Part-B** (>30% gender component) and **Part-C** (<30% gender component) schemes as compared to Part-A schemes (100% gender component).

The following **corrective measures** can be taken to reform the process of gender budgeting:
1. a) **Expansion of scope** to include areas having an inseparable gender component or indirect impact on women’s empowerment (e.g. unpaid care sector);
   b) **improvements in data collection** architecture to capture gendered nuances and issues of intersectionality;
   c) integration of **novel aspects** into gender budgeting (e.g. gender differential impact of taxation policies).

2. a) **Institutional capacity building** (e.g. strengthening gender budget cells, establishment of a centralized coordinating unit under WCD ministry);
   b) transparent **monitoring system**s to track fund utilisation;
   c) establishing linkages between allocations and outcomes (e.g. adoption of **Gender Impact Assessment**).

3. a) **Mainstreaming gender budgeting** across core ministries (e.g. transport) to achieve **cross-sectoral** **impact**;
   b) achieving greater **transparency in methodology**, especially in categorization between Parts A,B and C;
   c) **decentralized** (e.g. at local body level) and **participatory** budget-making (e.g. SHGs, NGOs)

**Implementation** of these reforms is important to move gender budgeting beyond **tokenistic allocations** towards **comprehensive gender-responsive governance**. This will mark a critical step in the direction of **women-led** **development**. (***444 words***)

**Additional reading** **The gender budget — bigger allocations, little impact - The Hindu** **Gender-Responsive Budgeting in India: A Stocktaking** **Gender Responsive Budgeting, as Fiscal Innovation: Evidence from India on “Processes” Lekha Chakraborty Working Paper No. 20**

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer with a brief overview of the UDAN scheme. <br>• In the body, first discuss the need to expand regional air connectivity in India by highlighting its intended benefits. <br>• Next, outline the achievements of the UDAN scheme and its role in enhancing regional air connectivity. <br>• Conclude by suggesting ways to fully realize the scheme's potential. |
| --- |

The **Ministry of Civil Aviation** launched the Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik **(UDAN) scheme in 2016** to enhance **regional** **air connectivity** from **unserved and underserved airports** in the country and make **air travel affordable to the** **masses**.

The scheme sought to expand **regional air connectivity** in India due to its following benefits:
1. a) **Cost optimization** through **shorter routes** and **efficient aircraft utilization**;
   b) serve the **growing** **demand for air-travel** due to **rise in disposable incomes** and **changing travel habits** (preference to save time, leisure travel);
   c) create **direct and indirect** **employment** opportunities (aircraft handling, ground operations, airport economy, MRO services).

2. a) Stimulate **inclusive economic growth** by attracting **investment** (multiplier effect) and promoting **trade**;
   b) boost **air connectivity** at pristine locations (e.g. Himalayan region, NE states) to attract **domestic and** **international tourism**;
   c) .

3. a) Reduce the **development deficit** between major metropolitan areas and Tier-2 and Tier-3 cities, thus addressing **regional disparity;**
   b) improve access to **basic services** in **remote areas** and enhance **government reach** in times of **emergency** (disasters);
   c) **social cohesion** and **national integration** through improved people-to-people connectivity across diverse regions.

4. a) Decongest** major airports, leading to better overall capacity and efficiency;**
   b) complement **other** **modes of transport** (rail, road) and **fill gaps** therein (difficulty in laying tracks due to terrain);
   c) **strategic** **utility** of regional air connectivity [linkage with border areas (Pasighat ALG in Arunachal Pradesh), ease of movement for troops and supplies].

In nearly **nine years** of its implementation, the UDAN scheme has **achieved several milestones:**
1. a) Under the Scheme, **625 routes** (Apr 2025) have been operationalized;
   b) a total of **90 airports**, including **15 heliports** and **2 water aerodromes** (Gujarat and Andaman and Nicobar Islands under UDAN 3.0) have been connected, benefitting nearly **1.5cr passengers.**.

2. a) Viability Gap Funding** (VGF) **of more than Rs. 4000 crore** has been disbursed to **11 operators** for route development;**
   b) facilitated **growth of civil aviation industry** (4 new operators since 2017);
   c) resulted in **optimum utilization of defence assets** by opening them up for **restricted civilian use** (civilian terminal at Hindon airbase)

3. a) UDAN 3.0 introduced **tourism routes** connecting destinations in the North-East**;**
   b) **UDAN 5.1** is dedicated to expanding **helicopter services in hilly regions;**
   c) prominent destinations, significant for **religious tourism** have been connected (Deoghar, Amritsar, Ajmer).

4. a) Escalated the **demand for aircrafts** of different types (seaplanes, 3-seat propeller planes);
   b) **UDAN 5.0** has been launched with a focus on Category-2 (20-80 seats) and Category-3 aircrafts (>80 seats);
   c) **UDAN** **5.2** has a focus on small aircrafts (<20 seats).

5. The **Krishi UDAN Scheme** was launched in 2020 for cost-effective and time-bound transportation of Agri- produce (originating especially from Northeast, hilly and tribal regions), thus helping farmers improve their **value realization**.

The recent **Ahmedabad plane crash (June 2025**) has put the spotlight on the issue of safety of air travel. As the scheme **continues to evolve (**Modified UDAN Scheme announced in the Union Budget 2025-26 for 10 years**),**

addressing the **safety challenges**, apart from those of **route utilization** and **passenger services** will be crucial for **realizing the full potential** of regional air connectivity in India. ***(484 words)***

