   c) heritage loss (sacred groves). occasional small fires reduce fuel load availability 2.
   a) Loss of biodiversity due to repeated fires (only for larger fires. fire hardy species remain);
   b) increased susceptibility to floods, landslides, due to burning

of ground cover and undergrowth; c) death of wildlife (including keystone, endangered species) and loss of habitat; d) loss of life among forest personnel and disruption of forest-based livelihoods (NTFP collection, tourism).

The following measures can be taken for their prevention, mitigation and management:
1. a) Vulnerability assessment through hazard risk zonation (history of fires, human settlements in the area) and capacity evaluation (equipment and manpower audit);
   b) development of early warning systems (MODIS, SNPP-VIIRS);
   c) deployment of information dissemination mechanisms.

2. a) Strengthening community-based systems (JFM);
   b) prioritization of fire control mechanisms in forestry projects;
   c) awareness generation (mock drills) and capacity building (protective suits);
   d) incorporation of gender specific and disability sensitive measures.

3. a) Deliberate lighting of smaller fires to control the main fire (backfire, fire line, controlled burning of biomass);
   b) near real-time monitoring through low flying aircrafts, drones;
   c) deployment of special forces for dousing fires (IAF-Bambi buckets), rescue and rehabilitation efforts;
   d) development of risk transfer mechanisms (insurance).

4. a) Accelerated plantation of fire resistant and native species (e.g. Dicliptera polymorpha);
   b) use of technological tools for fire management (e.g. modelling of fire path, understanding wind patterns);
   c) strict enforcement of legal provisions (Section 26, Indian Forest Act 1927)

ISFR 2023 reports a significant decline in the incidents of forest fires (from ~2.12 lakh during 2022-23 to ~2.03 lakh during 2023-24). This highlights the importance of continued interventions under the National Action Plan on Forest Fires (2018) and the NPFFM to sustain the trend. (531 words)

ICFRE: Indian Council of Forestry Research and Education; JFM: Joint Forest Management; ISFR: India State of Forest Report; OHT: Overhead Transmission; NTFP: Non-Timber Forest Produce; MODIS: Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectro- radiometer; SNPP-VIIRS: Suomi National Polar–orbiting Partnership- Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite; NPFFM: National Programme on Forest Fire Management

